  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Humanitarian Emergency Response to the Affected Populations of the Floods in Rio Grande do Sul State in Brazil</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Since the end of April 2024, heavy rains in the southern state of Rio Grande do Sul in Brazil have been causing devastating floods in at least 478 of the 497 municipalities across the state. According to the Brazilian Civil Defense, as of 10 June, more than 2.3 million people have been affected, more than 629,000 people are displaced (422,753 evacuees plus 16,128 in shelters), at least 173 people have been confirmed dead, and more than 30 are currently missing. A state of public calamity has been declared until December 2024, as the region faces a Level III disaster, due to the extreme climate events caused by heavy rains, floods, hailstorms, flash floods and intense winds. Through the deployment of a multi-sectoral mobile team, the International Organization for Migration in Brazil is providing assistance to flood-affected populations in Ro Grande do Sul by supporting local partners with technical support on camp management, information management, and direct support in the form of protection, mental health and psychosocial support, as well as conducting data collection and analysis to further inform the response.</narrative>
  </description>
